course: dictionary
lesson: 11949
title: Carbonise: when heat turns things black
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Carbonise is a verb, so it describes an action. To carbonise something is to heat it strongly, usually with very little air, until it turns into carbon or charcoal. Carbon is the black stuff left behind. «The toast was left under the grill and carbonised.» The toast did not just get warm. It became black and hard, so carbonise means turned to black carbon by heat.
explain: Carbonise can also be a slow process. In science, dead plants can be carbonised over a very long time, changing into coal or a thin black layer. «Ferns that died long ago were carbonised into coal.» The heat and pressure work for a very long time, not for one afternoon.
explain: Sometimes people use carbonise as a joke about cooking. Dad says, «The sausages are carbonised!» He does not mean they were turned into science material. He means they are burnt black and hard. Look at the clues around the word, and you will know which meaning fits.
fact: Carbonise is a verb: carbonise, carbonises, carbonised, carbonising.
fact: «Carbonise» means to turn something into carbon or charcoal by heating it strongly, usually without much air.
fact: In science, «carbonised» plants can become coal over a very long time.
fact: In cooking, a joking use of «carbonised» means burnt black and hard.
fact: In American spelling, the same word is «carbonize».
exercise: «The bread was left in the oven too long and carbonised.» What does carbonised mean here? | Decorated with sugar | *Burnt black and hard by heat | Soaked in cold water | Frozen solid in the freezer
exercise: «Plants that died long ago were slowly carbonised into coal.» Which best describes how this happened? | By being frozen in ice overnight | *Slowly, over a very long time, with heat and pressure | In one hot afternoon in a bonfire | By being washed away in a flood
exercise: «The carbonised wood crumbled in her hand.» What does carbonised suggest about the wood? | *It was burnt by heat and turned black | It was newly painted | It was wet and green
test: Dad says, «The sausages are carbonised!» What is he joking about? | They are cold and raw in the middle | They are far too sweet | *They are burnt black and hard | They are being sold at the shop
test: Which word is the past tense of carbonise? | carbonises | *carbonised | carbon-free | carbonising
exam: Mia says «carbonised» and «burnt» mean the same. Why might a science book choose carbonised? | It is a shorter word and so it is easier | It means the thing was painted black | *It shows that heat and time turned something into carbon, not just burnt it | It means the same as frozen
